    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the number of ways 9 people can sit around a round table where rotations of the same arrangement are considered identical, we use the concept of circular permutations.\n\nFor a round table, there are n-1 circular permutations where n is the number of people. Personally, I often find it convenient to compute the total permutations n!, then divide by the total possible rotations. \n\nn! is the factorial of n, which is the product of all positive integers up to n. It's a useful technique when you need to consider all possible permutations and minimize the number of differing positions.\n\nHowever, when it comes to circular permutations, things simplify because each seat is unique due to the rotational symmetry.\n\nSo, in a round table:\n- Instead of dividing by (n-1), we do divide by n.\n  \nThus, the number of circular permutations of n things is n!\n\nThis makes a total (n-1) * n! possibilities instead of n^n.\n\nTherefore, the number of distinct seating positions of 9 people around a round table remains (9-1) * 9!, or combinatorially (8 * 9!). It results in 72 packs.",
